WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.532 --> 00:00:03.920
Speaker 1: But don't worry, it's not&nbsp;
just major corporations that are guilty&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:03.920 --> 00:00:08.240
of greed here in the United States.&nbsp;
Congress has a very serious problem.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.680 --> 00:00:17.120
With using their insider knowledge to buy and&nbsp;
sell stocks. We have seen the reports, you know,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.120 --> 00:00:22.000
a Business Insider earlier this year or&nbsp;
maybe even early last year, whatever it was,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.000 --> 00:00:30.080
64 members of Congress in the last two years&nbsp;
have violated the STOCK Act. Almost 200 of their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.080 --> 00:00:38.160
staffers have also violated the STOCK Act. And&nbsp;
of course, these individuals have information.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.880 --> 00:00:43.840
They've got budget information. They know where&nbsp;
money's going to be spent before it gets spent.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.560 --> 00:00:50.560
And they can use this information. To buy&nbsp;
stocks. They can use it to sell stocks.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.480 --> 00:01:00.080
And a majority of Americans, 70% of them, to be&nbsp;
exact, say they want a ban on congressional stock&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.080 --> 00:01:07.280
trading. 50% of Republicans and 45% of Democrats&nbsp;
say they're actually going to be more likely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.280 --> 00:01:17.200
to vote for a candidate if they support a ban on&nbsp;
politicians trading stocks. I personally, you may&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.200 --> 00:01:22.000
think it's a little extreme. I want to go further&nbsp;
than just banning them, buying and selling stocks.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.280 --> 00:01:28.880
I don't think they should be allowed to own them.&nbsp;
Sorry again. That's just me. You can disagree.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.880 --> 00:01:34.560
That's fine. Doesn't matter. Doesn't bother me.&nbsp;
But you're choosing a life of public service.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.200 --> 00:01:41.040
You've got to give those things up. They&nbsp;
made Jimmy Carter sell his peanut farm.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.840 --> 00:01:46.880
Now he had to sell his peanut farm.&nbsp;
I don't think it's too far fetched to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.880 --> 00:01:49.760
say that members of Congress should&nbsp;
have to sell their Raytheon stock.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.200 --> 00:01:58.160
Not a blind trust. I want it banned. They&nbsp;
can't own it at all. But I would also settle&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.160 --> 00:02:03.280
if this is the best case scenario with a ban&nbsp;
on them buying and selling it. And so what,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.280 --> 00:02:09.440
of course, 70% of the American public. And the&nbsp;
biggest problem is this stock act that all these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.440 --> 00:02:13.760
congresspeople keep violating, Democrats and&nbsp;
Republicans alike. It's a bipartisan issue.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.040 --> 00:02:21.840
You know, the penalty is for violating&nbsp;
it. $200 fine if the fine is even,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.400 --> 00:02:28.960
you know, put in place, sometimes they waive the&nbsp;
fine. So you could trade $50,000 worth of stock.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.680 --> 00:02:36.320
You could buy 50,000, you know,&nbsp;
profit, ten, 20, 30%, whatever it is.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.320 --> 00:02:44.560
You can make ten, 20, $30,000 off of it.&nbsp;
And the only penalty you have is a $200 fine&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.120 --> 00:02:51.600
for violating the STOCK Act at that point. That's&nbsp;
not a fine. That's just a fee for doing business.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.720 --> 00:03:33.280
Listen, that's all the time we have for&nbsp;
